</section>

<section id="short" class="part">
<h2>In short</h2>
<ol class="points">
<li><p><strong>What failed.</strong> On 26 October a run of the clarity workflow rewrote 13 POSITIONS entries. You rejected every text. The clarity workflow is the tool that rewrites record entries so that each entry reads on its own and keeps its meaning.</p></li>
<li><p><strong>Why.</strong> The steps up to the run were right. The coordinator wrote a brief for the run, the instructions that the run worked from. That brief made a worker's list binding over the tool's own rules. On 29 October the post-mortem found a deeper cause. The coordinator took a worker's report as the plan. No judgement stood between the report and the action.</p></li>
<li><p><strong>What was done.</strong> At your word, the coordinator restored the tool and gave it an input for the review's findings. The review is the phase 3 exit review, which section 1 describes. A finding is a point that the review raised about an entry. The corrections ran first as a pilot of four entries. A pilot is a small first run. They then ran as two chunks of seven entries each. A chunk is a group of entries. Your reading found the next fault. Every finding had gone into the entry's text. An Opus worker then sorted the findings by home and by weight. A finding's home is the place in the record where it belongs. Its weight is how much it matters. One Opus writer wrote the changes from your comments. The changes landed, that is, entered the main branch, at 13:02 on 29 October.</p></li>
<li><p><strong>What it taught.</strong> A small pilot that you read was the only check of form that worked. Each finding has a home, and the entry's text is only one of them. A review that reports every fault needs a sort by significance before anyone writes a text.</p></li>
<li><p><strong>What is open.</strong> Part 2 of the salvage is open. The salvage is the set of small, targeted corrections that keep the review's sound work. Three of your answers of the evening are not yet in the record. The changes to the skills, the instruction files that the sessions load, wait on their branch. Some upkeep remains. Section 7 lists it all.</p></li>
</ol>
</section>

<section id="s1" class="part">
<h2><span class="num">Section 1</span>What happened on 26 and 27 October</h2>
<ol class="points">
<li><p>The phase 3 exit review is the review that you ordered on 24 October, before the switch-over. Phase 3 is the phase of the work that comes before the switch-over. The switch-over is the step at which the compiled path starts to use the interpreter's library. The compiled path compiles a program to bytecode and runs it. On 26 October the review's foundations round checked the nine tenets against the team's sources. The nine tenets are the project's base decisions. The team is the Sun Labs designers of Fortress.</p></li>
<li><p>At 07:42 you approved changes to two entries. P-37 holds that there is one library, the interpreter's. P-26 holds that the team's later word wins over the specification's early text. You said: “Yes, to both rewording of or refinements of P37 and P26. But we have like a clarity process workflow that you can use for writing it with a code reader.” You dictated that message, so “code reader” stands for “cold reader”, a reader with no context. At 07:54 you agreed to the wider plan for the entries that the review's judgement would flag: “Yeah, this sounds like a solid plan.”</p></li>
<li><p>An automatic compaction came at 08:29. After it, the coordinator wrote a run brief of 7,700 characters for 13 entries. It also added a hook to the tool's script, so that the run brief overrode the tool's own briefs. The tool's own briefs are the instructions that the tool gives its agents. The run brief said: “Where they differ, this brief wins.” The coordinator launched the run at 08:38.</p></li>
<li><p>At 19:08 you rejected the texts: “It is in the Claude speak. It is not in the ASD-STE100 register. It is not written as a timeless position.” The run wrote about 2.7 million tokens. The estimate was 1.5 million.</p></li>
<li><p>On 27 October you asked to keep the review's sound work as small, targeted corrections. The record calls these corrections the salvage. You refused a new process: “No. No go. The plan seems to reinvent something for which we already have workflows.” At your word, the coordinator removed the hook.</p></li>
<li><p>On 28 October at 07:37 you said: “We already invented this process. We perfected it. It worked well during the last record consolidation. We have probably failed to encode how to use it correctly next time.” A record consolidation is a pass that rewrites the record's entries. At 07:42 you asked: “Our previous record consolidation was a success. This last one was an utter failure. What went wrong?”</p></li>
</ol>
