<section id="s2" class="part">
<h2><span class="num">Section 2</span>What the post-mortem found</h2>
<ol class="points">
<li><p><strong>You corrected the first account twice.</strong> At 09:56 you said that the question of the skills starts at the last good consolidation, not at 23 October. At 10:28 you said: “the events that happened here were all approved and correct, even in retrospect. We are analyzing what had bent the briefing that the coordinator produced.” The post-mortem revised the account after each correction.</p></li>
<li><p><strong>The causes, heaviest first.</strong></p>
<ol>
<li>The Fable worker that judged the review had listed, for each entry, what the new text must make the coordinator do. The run brief made that list binding over the clarity writer's rule “Keep the meaning.” The clarity writer is the tool's step that writes the new text. The list carried batch numbers, counts and dates. The new texts grew to 1.9 times the size of the entries, and they carried history.</li>
<li>The coordinator wrote the brief at a fresh boot, eight minutes after a compaction. Its context then held 466 thousand tokens. It wrote the brief from the plan in the boot note. The boot note is the file that tells the coordinator, after a compaction or a stop, what is in flight and what to do next. Nobody checked the brief against the record's rules for an entry. The brief set no length cap and no register.</li>
<li>The skills named no tool for correcting entries after a review. The lessons that would have stopped the run stayed in four other places: a tool's brief, a README, a commit message and the held list. The held list is the file of points that you raised while reading.</li>
<li>Nothing caught the fault before the end. There was no pilot and no length check. The trap test asks whether a reader who acts as the coordinator decides right from the entries. It checks decisions only. It scored the rejected texts 12 of 12.</li>
<li>After the rejection, the coordinator proposed four ways forward in two days. The last was an ask that you could not read. At 05:56 on 28 October you said: “No. None of this. I cannot decipher what you are proposing here.”</li>
</ol></li>
<li><p><strong>The skills between the last good consolidation and the failed one</strong> were right but too thin. Fourteen commits say what each tool does. They do not say which job each tool fits.</p></li>
<li><p><strong>The deeper cause.</strong> At 09:12 on 29 October you asked whether you had been “prompting a genie”. The coordinator answered at once: “The genie did not follow your wish literally. It followed a worker's report literally, and your words only as far as they named the tool.” It added: “The same pattern ran yesterday, with me. The account's worker proposed three tool changes; I relayed them to you without checking the tools.” You had seen that at 11:49 on 28 October: “you are committing the very same mistake that the original the Opus in full context when it caused the debacle.”</p></li>
<li><p><strong>Your other asks of 10:39 on 28 October.</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>The Haiku window ran from 16 to 19 October. In those days the coordinator ran on the Haiku model by accident. The Haiku window did not produce the weakened export check of row 816 of the gap ledger. The export check compares a component with its interface file. The gap ledger is the file of known gaps. Nor did the window produce the entries that the review found bent. All of them came later, under Opus. At 11:00 you accepted that: “Okay, so that was my misremembering.” You then asked who had raised row 816. The recheck found that your memory held in part. The batch's own reviewer raised row 816 on 23 October. Climb batch 22 then launched overnight at its default, and nobody sent you the question. A climb batch is a Workflow run of several code tasks that land together.</li>
<li>The exit review's records are one process in ten places. At 11:17 you said: “So yes, let's do the workers proposal. And create a README.” A README now names the process's six layers. Its open asks moved into the review queue as items 279 to 290. The review queue is the file of items that wait for your word.</li>
<li>One ask has no answer yet: whether the coordinator's delegation declined since the early Fable sessions. You placed it “once we recover from here”.</li>
</ul></li>
</ol>
